PQC PROBLEMS TO SOLVE:

Most discussions about Post-Quantum Cryptography (PQC) focus entirely on algorithms.
​When people start planning for PQC, they assume the hardest part is picking the right NIST standard or switching a library.
​That isn't what surprises you when you actually touch the infrastructure.
​Replacing a primitive inside an isolated app is straightforward.
​The real mess begins when you try to achieve enterprise-scale visibility. You realize very quickly that you don't actually know what exists across your perimeter.

​You aren't just looking at a clean database. The engineering reality is complete chaos.
​You start digging into the infrastructure and find:
​Hardcoded cipher suites buried inside legacy internal binaries.
​Shadow IT services running undocumented API endpoints.

​Third-party vendor appliances running proprietary, locked-down TLS stacks.
​Ephemeral container workloads spinning up with custom, unmapped OpenSSL forks.
​At this stage, you realize that basic network scanning is a joke. It only captures what's active right at that second.

​To get true visibility, the questions shift from network layers to runtime and static engineering:
​Can your SAST rules reliably flag non-compliant curves in custom source code without blowing up your triage pipeline?
​How do you hook into cryptographic providers at the JVM or CLR level to map active handshakes without killing production performance?

​Can you reliably intercept system calls using eBPF at the kernel level to see what legacy systems are doing in the background?
​If you try to migrate without solving this visibility puzzle first, you aren't executing a strategy.
​You are just guessing.

​And guessing at enterprise scale means introducing massive, unmapped operational risks.
​Discovery isn't a compliance checklist item. It is the absolute core foundation of cryptographic lifecycle management.
​I'm curious whether others working on infrastructure architecture are relying on deep runtime instrumentation, or if you're stuck trying to clean up network-level scans.

