Shor and Grover are not the same quantum threat.

This is where many PQC discussions get confused.

Both are important.

But they impact cryptography in very different ways.

Shor’s algorithm is the bigger threat to today’s public-key cryptography.

It targets the mathematical assumptions behind:

• RSA
• Diffie-Hellman
• Elliptic Curve Cryptography

That matters because public-key cryptography is deeply embedded in digital trust:

• TLS
• certificates
• digital signatures
• software updates
• VPNs
• identity systems
• telecom security
• banking infrastructure

So when people say “quantum computers can break today’s cryptography,” they are mostly referring to Shor’s impact on public-key systems.

Grover’s algorithm is different.

It speeds up brute-force search against symmetric cryptography.

That does not mean AES is dead.

It means key-size planning and security margins become important.

A simple mental model:

Shor threatens public-key trust.
Grover affects symmetric key-strength planning.

This distinction matters.

Because PQC migration is mainly urgent due to Shor’s impact on RSA, ECC, key exchange, and signatures.

Grover matters too — but it does not create the same kind of public-key trust disruption.

For enterprises, telecom networks, banks, governments, and critical infrastructure, misunderstanding this difference can lead to the wrong migration strategy.

Quantum-safe readiness starts with asking the right question:

Which cryptographic assumptions are at risk — and how deeply are they embedded in our systems?

Shubham: We should worry of the threat of circumventing the cryptology with hashtag#AI-Accelerated Brute-Force search, which is distinct from quantum.

Stronger key-size planning cannot really mitigate it, nor does hashtag#NIST’s hashtag#PQC.

Pattern-Devoid-Cryptography (PDC) is a counter measure.

 The key point is that Shor and Grover create very different migration challenges. Grover mainly affects symmetric key-strength planning and security margins. Shor is more disruptive because it challenges the public-key trust fabric behind PKI, TLS, VPNs, device identity, software signing, and telecom/banking trust chains.

That is why PQC readiness cannot be treated as a simple algorithm swap. It requires crypto discovery, ownership, migration policy, coexistence planning, and a crypto-agile operating model.

